    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d921">

                <pb facs="1065" n="947" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg947"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.4112A/29: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Houghton</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-10-17T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 17,
                                1928—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>231. Instruction No. 1437 of May 22, 1928. Embassy’s No. 88, April 27, 1928, 2
                        p.m.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d921-f1"/> Arbitration Treaty.</p>
                <p>The suggestion of the British Ambassador that the Arbitration Treaty of 1908,
                    which expired June 4, should be prolonged proved <pb facs="1066" n="948"
                        xml:id="pg948"/> impracticable and nothing has since been received from the
                    British Government in regard to negotiation of new treaty.</p>
                <p>Please inquire at the Foreign Office whether it will shortly furnish the British
                    Embassy in Washington with instructions. An early reply is desired.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Clark</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
